[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise.
They were merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and
every one of them has a subject about a wrong date. Read the bodies, place each report by its
tenant and the way it came in, and three problems come out. The queued parser fix closes one.

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix does not reach the other two. Each now has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## How each report was placed

- **Read the body, not the subject.** Subjects were picked from the same handful of phrases for
  every problem, so they cannot split one.
- **Tenant, not browser.** The cause depends on the tenant's locale and booking form
  (`causes.ts`, `locales.ts`). Intake's tag guesses from the reporter's browser, and it is wrong
  for dozens of these reports.
- **A flipped month is not a one-day move.** The desk already listed five reports that read as
  one day off (`desk-notes.json`). [The #4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) still counts them under
  `parser` or `hint` by tenant. No swap of day and month gives one day off, and one report
  shows the same meeting on two days in two time zones. They are their own problem.
- **One id, one report.** The export repeats some reports across pages; each counts once.

## What #4471 shows today, for comparison

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- Merging on subject made one ticket out of three problems. A count on a merged ticket counts
  subjects, not bugs.
- "Known" closed the question for the two problems nobody had looked at.

